<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Staffordshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Staffordshire is a county in the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> as its county town. The area lay in the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Mercia, whose bishopric was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> and whose wealth is reflected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire-hoard">Staffordshire Hoard</a> found in 2009. In the industrial era the Potteries around Stoke-on-Trent made it the centre of English ceramics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/staffordshire.jpg" alt="Staffordshire" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Rayner · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stoke-on-Trent_Bottle_Kiln_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6995.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Staffordshire is a ceremonial county in the West Midlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicestershire">Leicestershire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a> and the West Midlands county. It covers 1,713 square kilometres, with Stoke-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> in the north as its largest settlement and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> in the centre. The north is hilly, with the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> and part of the Peak District National Park, and Cannock Chase lies in the south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-trent">River Trent</a> and its tributaries drain most of the county. The village of Flash, at 463 metres, is the highest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county contains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> tumuli and Roman camps. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> settled it in the sixth century, and the oldest example of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> knot, the county's symbol, appears on an Anglian cross in the churchyard of Stoke Minster. The county was formed in the early tenth century, when Stafford became the capital of Mercia. In July 2009 the largest hoard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> gold ever found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was discovered in a field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire-hoard">Staffordshire Hoard</a> has been tentatively dated to the 7th or 8th centuries, in the time of the Kingdom of Mercia.</p><p>In 1553 Queen Mary made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> a county corporate, which kept it administered separately until 1888. The county industrialised rapidly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, when the North Staffordshire coalfield was exploited and fuelled iron and motor industries in the south. Pottery became its most best-known product, and in 1910 the six towns of the Staffordshire Potteries became the single county borough of Stoke-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a>. Hugh Bourne, a native of Stoke-on-Trent, founded Primitive Methodism at a gathering at Mow Cop, and by 1811 he and his brother had founded the first chapel in the Tunstall area.</p><p>The historic county included much of the present West Midlands, including Walsall, West Bromwich and Wolverhampton. On 1 April 1974 under the Local Government Act 1972 the Black Country county boroughs and the Aldridge-Brownhills urban district became part of the new metropolitan county of West Midlands. On 1 April 1997 Stoke-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> became a unitary authority independent of Staffordshire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county's only cathedral is Lichfield Cathedral, in the city of Lichfield. Burton upon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> is known for brewing, and Stoke-on-Trent still produces a limited amount of pottery, including the work of Wedgwood and Royal Doulton. Keele University and the University of Staffordshire are in the county. The Trent and Mersey Canal and the other canals of the county form an extensive network, and the West Coast Main Line passes through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Staffordshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mercia, from the Old English Mierce, the 'border people', took shape among Anglian settlers in the upper Trent valley, around what later became <b>Staffordshire</b>, Derbyshire and Leicestershire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A copper alloy pan of the 2nd century, found in <b>Staffordshire</b> in 2003, is inscribed with the names of four forts on the western sector of the wall, followed by words that most likely read as 'Hadrian's Wall', though they may instead name a man called Aelius Draco.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Versions close to the one now famous were recorded from Emily Bishop of Bromsberrow Heath, Gloucestershire, in 1952, Dorothy Davey of Hull in 1969, George Dunn of Quarry Bank, <b>Staffordshire</b>, in 1971 and Frank Hinchliffe of Sheffield in 1976.</blockquote><a href="/songs/here-we-come-a-wassailing">Here We Come A-wassailing</a></li><li><blockquote>Various sources place this version in different parts of England during the 19th century, and several versions survived into the 20th, when folk song collectors recorded them from George Dunn and Mary Evans of Quarry Bank, <b>Staffordshire</b> (both in 1971), and Miss J. Howman of Stow-on-the-Wold, Gloucestershire (1966).</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-wish-you-a-merry-christmas">We Wish You a Merry Christmas</a></li><li><blockquote>At Blore Heath in <b>Staffordshire</b>, near Market Drayton, the Yorkists won a battle of the Wars of the Roses, the struggle between the houses of Lancaster and York for the English throne that lasted until 1487.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-23">23 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cornovii occupied Shropshire, Cheshire and parts of <b>Staffordshire</b> and the Welsh borderland in the late Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornovii">Cornovii</a></li></ul></section>
